<!DOCTYPE topic
        SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       title="JirAI: Work with Jira issues" id="Work-with-Jira-Issues">

    <primary-label ref="jirai"/>
    <show-structure for="chapter,procedure" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>Open: <ui-path>View | Tool Windows | JirAI</ui-path></p>
        <p>Open an issue: select it and press <shortcut>Enter</shortcut></p>
        <p>Create a branch: right-click an issue and choose <control>Create Branch…</control></p>
        <p>Branch settings: <ui-path>Settings | Tools | JirAI | Development</ui-path></p>
        <p>Requires: signed in to a Jira Cloud site</p>
    </tldr>

    <link-summary>Find issues in the JirAI tool window, act on them, and create Git branches and commits that carry the issue key.</link-summary>
    <card-summary>Search, filter and act on Jira issues, and branch and commit from them.</card-summary>
    <web-summary>Browse Jira Cloud issues in the JirAI tool window of JetBrains IDEs: search, filter and sort, copy issue links, create a Git branch from an issue, and see the current issue in the status bar.</web-summary>

    <p>The <control>JirAI</control> tool window lists your Jira issues next to your code.</p>
    <p>Use it to find an issue, open its details and timeline, and run common actions on it.</p>
    <p>In JirAI, <emphasis>issue</emphasis> and <emphasis>work item</emphasis> mean the same thing: Jira Cloud calls issues work items in its newer screens, and some JirAI views use that term too.</p>

    <chapter title="The JirAI tool window" id="tool-window">
        <p>The <control>JirAI</control> tool window opens on the left side of the IDE.</p>
        <p>On the IDE start screen, where no project is open, click <control>More</control> on the left tool window bar and choose <control>JirAI</control>.</p>
        <p>Its <control>Jira</control> tab holds the issue list, and each issue you open adds a details tab named after the issue key.</p>
        <p>The tool window title bar has two buttons:</p>
        <deflist type="narrow">
            <def title="+ (New Issue)">Opens the Create form. See <a href="Jira-Create-Issues.topic">Create issues</a>.</def>
            <def title="Space Summary">Opens a Jira space with its summary, board, list and reports. See <a href="JirAI-Metrics.topic">Spaces</a>.</def>
        </deflist>
        <img src="jirai_overview.png" alt="JirAI tool window with the issue list beside an issue&#x27;s Timeline tab" width="706" thumbnail="true" border-effect="rounded"/>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Search and sort" id="search-and-sort">
        <list>
            <li><p>Type in the <control>Search issues</control> field to match issue text.</p></li>
            <li>
                <p>Type an issue key on its own, for example <code>DEMO-123</code>, to find that issue whatever its age.</p>
                <p>The issue must still match the other active filters.</p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Click <control>Sort By</control> (the sort icon) and choose <control>Updated</control>, <control>Created</control> or <control>Priority</control>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Toggle <control>Descending</control> in the same menu to reverse the order.</p></li>
            <li><p>More issues load as you scroll down the list.</p></li>
        </list>
        <note>
            <p>Searches and filters cover issues created in the last 365 days.</p>
            <p>To open an older issue, type its key.</p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Filter the list" id="filters">
        <procedure title="Apply a quick filter" id="quick-filters-proc">
            <step><p>Click <control>Filters</control> (the funnel icon) above the list.</p></step>
            <step>
                <p>Under <control>Quick Filters</control>, choose <control>Assigned to Me</control>, <control>Unassigned</control> or <control>All Issues</control>.</p>
                <img src="jirai_quick_filters_sort.png" alt="Filters menu with the Quick Filters group and Assigned to Me selected" width="177" border-effect="rounded"/>
            </step>
        </procedure>
        <p>The first time you open the tool window in a project, the list uses the <control>Assigned to Me</control> quick filter and shows the most recently updated issues first.</p>
        <p>To see other people's issues and unassigned issues too, choose <control>All Issues</control>.</p>
        <p>To narrow the list further, use the filter chips: <control>Project</control>, <control>Epic</control>, <control>Status</control>, <control>Assignee</control>, <control>Reporter</control>, <control>Type</control> and <control>Priority</control>.</p>
        <list>
            <li><p>Click a value once to include it.</p></li>
            <li><p>Click it a second time, or <shortcut>Shift</shortcut>+click it, to exclude it; the chip then reads <control>not</control> followed by the value.</p></li>
            <li><p>Use <control>All</control> and <control>Clear</control> at the top of a chip popup to select or clear every value.</p></li>
            <li><p>The <control>Status</control> popup also offers <control>Unresolved</control>.</p></li>
            <li><p><control>Assignee</control> and <control>Reporter</control> accept several people.</p></li>
            <li>
                <p>The <control>Assignee</control> and <control>Reporter</control> popups have <control>Clear</control> but no <control>All</control>.</p>
                <p>You are pinned on top, under <control>Unassigned</control> and above a divider.</p>
            </li>
        </list>
        <img src="jirai_issue_filters.png" alt="Status filter popup with one value excluded and the All and Clear links" width="184" border-effect="rounded"/>
        <p>To remove every filter, choose <control>Clear Filters</control> in the <control>Filters</control> menu.</p>
        <p>When nothing matches, the list shows <control>Nothing matches the filters.</control> with a <control>Clear filters</control> link.</p>
        <p>JirAI remembers the filters and sort order for each IDE project.</p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Open an issue" id="open-issue">
        <p>Select an issue and press <shortcut>Enter</shortcut>, or double-click it.</p>
        <p>JirAI opens the issue's details tab in the tool window and its <control>Timeline</control> tab in the editor.</p>
        <p>See <a href="Jira-Issue-Details-and-Editing.topic">Issue details</a> and <a href="Jira-Timeline-and-Description-Editor.topic">Timeline and breakdown</a>.</p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Issue actions" id="row-actions">
        <p>Right-click an issue in the list to open its context menu:</p>
        <deflist type="medium">
            <def title="Open Details">Opens the details tab and the <control>Timeline</control> tab, as <shortcut>Enter</shortcut> does.</def>
            <def title="Open in Browser">Opens the issue on your Jira site.</def>
            <def title="Copy Issue URL">Copies the issue's Jira link, for example <code>https://demo-org.atlassian.net/browse/DEMO-123</code>, to the clipboard.</def>
            <def title="Copy Issue Key">Copies the issue key, for example <code>DEMO-123</code>, to the clipboard.</def>
            <def title="Create Branch…">Creates a Git branch for the issue. See <a anchor="create-branch">Create a branch from an issue</a>.</def>
            <def title="Refresh">Reloads the list from Jira. It uses the IDE's <control>Refresh</control> shortcut, which you can look up in <ui-path>Settings | Keymap</ui-path>.</def>
            <def title="Delete">Deletes the issue after a confirmation. See <a anchor="delete-issue">Delete an issue</a>.</def>
        </deflist>
        <img src="jirai_issue_context_menu.png" alt="Issue context menu with Open Details, Open in Browser, Copy Issue Key, Refresh and Delete" width="440" border-effect="rounded"/>
        <p>The issue lists in a space's <control>List</control> tab and in an issue's <control>Breakdown</control> tab have the same menu.</p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Delete an issue" id="delete-issue">
        <procedure title="Delete an issue" id="delete-issue-proc">
            <step><p>Right-click the issue and choose <control>Delete</control>.</p></step>
            <step>
                <p>Wait while the dialog checks your permissions and the issue's child work items.</p>
                <p>The dialog lists the sub-tasks and other children that would be affected.</p>
            </step>
            <step>
                <p>Type the issue key in the <control>Type DEMO-123 to confirm:</control> field and click <control>Delete</control>.</p>
                <img src="jirai_delete_dialog.png" alt="Delete dialog asking you to type the issue key to confirm" width="504" border-effect="rounded"/>
            </step>
        </procedure>
        <warning>
            <p>Deleting an issue cannot be undone, and everything attached to it is deleted with it.</p>
            <p>Watchers are notified.</p>
        </warning>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Create an issue" id="create-issue">
        <p>Click <control>+</control> in the tool window title bar to open the Create form.</p>
        <p>See <a href="Jira-Create-Issues.topic">Create issues</a>.</p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Create a branch from an issue" id="create-branch">
        <secondary-label ref="jirai_version"/>
        <p>JirAI names a Git branch after an issue and opens the IDE's own new-branch dialog.</p>
        <procedure title="Create a branch for an issue" id="create-branch-proc">
            <step>
                <p>Choose <control>Create Branch…</control> in one of these places:</p>
                <list>
                    <li><p>the context menu of an issue in the issue list;</p></li>
                    <li><p>the menu of a card on the <a href="JirAI-Board.topic">board</a>;</p></li>
                    <li><p>the right-click menu of the issue details tab.</p></li>
                </list>
            </step>
            <step>
                <p>In the <control>Create Branch for DEMO-42</control> dialog, check the suggested name, for example <code>DEMO-42-fix-login-timeout</code>.</p>
                <p>Change the name or the <control>Checkout branch</control> option if you need to.</p>
                <p>To start the branch from another local branch, a remote branch or a tag, select <control>Start from</control> and choose it.</p>
                <!-- planned-image src="jirai_create_branch_dialog.png" alt="New branch dialog with a name built from the issue key and summary" -->
            </step>
            <step><p>Click <control>Create</control>.</p></step>
        </procedure>
        <p>If a branch with the suggested name already exists, JirAI does not open the dialog.</p>
        <p>Instead, a notification says so and offers <control>Check Out</control>.</p>
        <p>A branch you create without checking it out also gets a <control>Check Out</control> notification.</p>

        <chapter title="Move the issue to In Progress" id="create-branch-in-progress">
            <p>After you check out the new branch, JirAI offers to move the issue to an In Progress status.</p>
            <list>
                <li><p>The first time, the notification lists every In Progress status the workflow allows, for example <control>In Progress</control> and <control>In Review</control>.</p></li>
                <li><p>JirAI remembers the status you pick for that Jira project.</p></li>
                <li><p>The next time, the notification asks <control>Branch created. Move DEMO-42 to In Progress?</control> with <control>Move</control>, <control>Change…</control> and <control>Don't Ask Again</control>.</p></li>
                <li><p>If the move needs fields, such as a resolution, the notification offers to open the issue.</p></li>
            </list>
            <p>JirAI makes no offer for an issue that is already in progress or done.</p>
        </chapter>

        <chapter title="Branch settings" id="create-branch-settings">
            <p>Open <ui-path>Settings | Tools | JirAI | Development</ui-path> to change the following:</p>
            <list>
                <li><p><control>Branch name:</control> the name template, <code>{key}-{summary}</code> by default. It can also use <code>{type}</code>.</p></li>
                <li><p><control>After creating a branch:</control> <control>Ask to move the issue to In Progress</control> (the default), <control>Move the issue to In Progress</control> or <control>Do nothing</control>.</p></li>
                <li><p><control>Forget remembered In Progress targets now</control>.</p></li>
            </list>
            <p>The template must contain <code>{key}</code>, and names are capped at 60 characters.</p>
            <p>See <a href="JirAI-Settings-Reference.topic">Settings reference</a>.</p>
        </chapter>
        <note>
            <p><control>Create Branch…</control> needs the IDE's bundled Git plugin.</p>
            <p>It is disabled when the project has no Git repository.</p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="The current issue in the status bar" id="current-issue-status-bar">
        <secondary-label ref="jirai_version"/>
        <p>When the name of your current Git branch contains an issue key, for example <code>DEMO-42-fix-login-timeout</code>, that issue is the <emphasis>current issue</emphasis>.</p>
        <list>
            <li><p>The status bar shows the current issue's key with the Jira icon.</p></li>
            <li><p>Hover over it to see the summary and status.</p></li>
            <li><p>Click it to open the issue's details tab in the <control>JirAI</control> tool window.</p></li>
            <li><p>When the project's repositories are on branches for different issues, the widget reads, for example, <control>DEMO-42 +1</control>, and a click lets you choose the issue.</p></li>
        </list>
        <p>To open the current issue from the keyboard, press <shortcut>Ctrl+Shift+A</shortcut> (macOS: <shortcut>⌘ ⇧ A</shortcut>) and run <control>Open Current Jira Issue</control>.</p>
        <p>The key must belong to an issue on the Jira site you are signed in to.</p>
        <p>To hide the widget, right-click the status bar and clear <control>JirAI Current Issue</control>.</p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="The issue key in commit messages" id="commit-message-issue-key">
        <secondary-label ref="jirai_version"/>
        <p>When there is a current issue, JirAI starts an empty commit message with its key, for example <code>DEMO-42 </code>.</p>
        <list>
            <li><p>This works when changelists are on, not with the Git staging area.</p></li>
            <li><p>JirAI never overwrites a draft commit message or a Git commit template.</p></li>
            <li><p>When the project's repositories are on branches for different issues, JirAI adds no key.</p></li>
        </list>
        <p>To turn it off, clear <control>Start commit messages with the current issue key</control> in <ui-path>Settings | Tools | JirAI | Development</ui-path>.</p>
    </chapter>

